# {{.ProjectName}}

A Nucleus module: its routes, the policy rows that open them, its typed
configuration and the service it provides to other modules travel together,
and an application gets all of them with one `Mount`.

Generated with `nucleus new {{.ProjectName}} --template module`, against
Nucleus {{.FrameworkVersion}}.

## Use it in an application

```bash
go get {{.Module}}
```

```go
import "{{.Module}}"

nucleus.New().
    FromConfigFile("nucleus.yml").
    Mount({{.PackageName}}.Module()).
    Start()
```

```yaml
# nucleus.yml — optional: the defaults apply without it
modules:
  {{.ModuleName}}:
    greeting: hello
```

`GET /{{.ModuleName}}/hello?name=Ada` answers `{"message":"hello, Ada"}`.

Another module of the application uses the `Greeter` this one provides: it
declares the dependency, so it starts after this module, and resolves the
value in its `OnStart`:

```go
nucleus.Module[struct{}]{
    Name:      "billing",
    DependsOn: []string{ {{- .PackageName}}.Name},
    OnStart: func(ctx context.Context, rt nucleus.Runtime, _ struct{}) error {
        greeter, err := nucleus.Resolve[*{{.PackageName}}.Greeter](rt)
        // keep greeter for the routes
        return err
    },
}.Build()
```

## Develop it

```bash
go test ./...
```

- `TestConformance` runs `nucleustest.CheckModule`: the checks the
  framework applies when an application mounts the module — name, prefix,
  configuration, policy rows, `OnStart`, routes, `OnShutdown` — each failing
  with its name and the framework's reason. A module that passes it mounts.
- `TestHello` and `TestProvidesTheGreeter` boot an in-process application
  with the module mounted and use it the way an application does.

The tests open a temporary SQLite database, which is why `go.mod` requires
the SQLite driver module; the module's own code links no driver — the
application that mounts it chooses its engine.

`.github/workflows/test.yml` runs `go vet` and `go test` on every push to
`main` and every pull request, with its actions pinned to a commit;
`.github/dependabot.yml` proposes the next pins and the next Nucleus
release.

Writing modules: https://jcsvwinston.github.io/quantum/nucleus/concepts/writing-a-module
